body:
> The patched methods currently don't survive when the script is reloaded. I wonder whether this might be a problem during the development in practice, because hot reloading can destroy the mod while developing it.

At least this shouldn't be a problem if you explicitly reload the script at runtime, resetting patched methods is the intended behavior. Regarding hot reloading, we can probably solve this by setting `keep_state = true`. Alternatively, plugin authors can disable hot reloading.

---

> What is the difference between the patch method and the .pck patch files?

Modding system authors want to support the simultaneous loading of multiple mods (chained), which can patch the same file or even the same method. PCK patches, on the other hand, work at the file/resource level. Some modding system authors have chosen the `take_over_path()` approach. See the diagram:

<img width="800" height="677" alt="" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/ab9e6279-5930-42cf-b700-4a53a56dc18c" />

However, in 4.0, this doesn't work with named classes and isn't guaranteed to work at all, as it depends on the loading order and dependency graph. If a scene/resource depends on a script, or if that script is required for static analysis, then it is loaded, and other scripts will depend on it. You won't be able to override it with a chain of other dynamically loaded scripts.

The alternative I propose differs in the following ways (compared to pure PCK patches and the current workaround in the form of an export plugin used by the ModLoader authors):

1. It's a more explicit feature than substitution using inheritance and `take_over_path()`.
2. It works at runtime, even after the class has already been loaded and its instances have been created. It doesn't require script reloading.
3. Other languages ​​can be used as wrappers (`patch_method()` accepts any `Callable`) and for function composition.
4. It can potentially be used not only for mods but also for other tasks (decorators for logging, testing, etc.).
5. It has a simple API, you don't need to parse and modify the script code to find functions and inner classes.
6. It has no runtime overhead for unpatched methods.

For example, in JavaScript, which is a very dynamic language, you can do the following:

```javascript
class MyClass {
    myMethod() {
        console.log(123);
    }
}

let x = new MyClass();
x.myMethod(); // Prints `123`.
MyClass.prototype.myMethod = function () {
    console.log(456);
};
x.myMethod(); // Prints `456`.
```

GDScript, despite being dynamically typed, is not as dynamic (not in terms of typing) as JavaScript and Python. You can't override function implementations or create properties on the fly (the `_set()` and `_get()` methods serve a similar purpose, but are a slightly different matter). On the one hand, this is good, as the static properties allow you to avoid many errors. On the other hand, it deprives you of some features found in dynamic languages.
